    "proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2), a=pi-1, and I(r)=(a*x,(y,Lambda22)) for a primitive vector r=(x,y). Reflection integrality is (q(r))|2I(r). Away from pi and 3 the spatial Gram is unimodular, hence no prime can divide q without contradicting primitivity. At the inert prime 3, the Smith factor 6 gives exponent at most one; if it occurs then G*y is divisible by 3. At pi, if x is a unit then q=-a*x^2 mod pi and the exponent is zero. Otherwise the even spatial form excludes exponent one. The Smith factors 2 and 6 give min(v_pi(I),2)<=2, so q|2I gives v_pi(q)<=4. Thus the exact dyadic exponents are among 0,2,3,4. Their totally-positive representatives are 1,2,4+2*pi,4; multiplication by 3 gives the four 3-primary classes. At exponent 3, G*y divisible by pi means G*u is even for y=u+pi*v. At exponent 4, G*y divisible by pi^2 means both G*u and G*v are even. For the 3-primary classes both G*u and G*v are divisible by 3. Empty classes remain part of the complete alphabet, so no realization assumption enters the proof.",
